<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Thomas Carleton</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1735–<a href="/years/1817">1817</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Thomas Carleton (c. 1735–1817) was an Irish-born British army officer who became the first governor of New <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brunswick">Brunswick</a> when it was separated from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nova-scotia">Nova Scotia</a> in 1784 to receive Loyalist refugees. He chose <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fredericton">Fredericton</a> as the capital and set up the colony's institutions. He left for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> in 1803 but formally held the post until his death.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Carleton was born in Strabane, County Tyrone, in Ulster, the son of Catherine Ball and the younger brother of Guy Carleton, later 1st Baron Dorchester. From a military family, he joined the British Army young and was an ensign in the 20th Regiment of Foot by 1753. He served with it in the Seven Years' War and afterwards watched the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russo-turkish-war-1768-1774">Russo-Turkish War</a> of 1768–1774 as an observer.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>In 1776, during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/american-revolutionary-war">American Revolutionary War</a>, he arrived at Quebec City as a lieutenant colonel with forces to relieve his brother, then Governor General of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canada">Canada</a>, whom Continental Army troops were besieging there. After Lieutenant Colonel Patrick Gordon died on 25 July 1776 he was given command of the 29th Regiment of Foot. He quarrelled with Frederick Haldimand, who replaced his brother as governor general in 1778, and in 1782 refused Haldimand's demand that he resign as quartermaster general. He returned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> and lodged with Lord Shelburne, then First Lord of the Treasury.</p><p>In the summer of 1784 he took charge of the new province of New Brunswick and helped resettle the many Loyalists leaving the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>. He became a major-general on 12 October 1793. In the last years of the century he survived a censure motion and an attempt to remove him led by the assemblyman James Glenie, whom a court-martial including Carleton had convicted in 1780 of conduct unbecoming an officer and a gentleman.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>After he sailed for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> in 1803 he never returned. When the Chesapeake–Leopard affair of 1807 raised tension with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>, he was ordered back to his post, but refused because the new governor general, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/james-henry-craig">Sir James Henry Craig</a>, was a lieutenant-general while he was a full general, and his duties fell to a succession of local officers. He died on 2 February 1817. He and his wife Hannah Foy had a son, William, and daughters Emma and Anne. Mount Carleton, the highest mountain in New Brunswick, is named after him.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Thomas Carleton” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Wanting a government of their own, they won partition in 1784, and <b>Thomas Carleton</b> became the first governor.</blockquote><a href="/state/colony-of-new-brunswick">Colony of New Brunswick</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Ireland. Died in Ramsgate, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-kingdom">the United Kingdom</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=53.35,-7.92&amp;year=1788" aria-label="Ireland on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Thomas Carleton"><image href="/maps/globes/g000n60.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4597 5543h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4597 5543h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=53.35,-7.92&amp;year=1788">What was here in 1788? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
